-- | OpenZeppelin Pausable - an emergency-stop switch for Daml templates.
--
-- The Daml translation of `Pausable.sol`. In Solidity, `Pausable` is inherited:
-- the flag is a field of the contract that it protects, and gated functions
-- carry the `whenNotPaused` modifier. The Daml analogue of that inheritance is
-- an interface instance, so this package defines the interfaces and the guards,
-- and the implementing template holds the flag.
--
-- Keeping the flag on the protected template is what makes the guard sound. The
-- state cannot be substituted, because it is not a separate contract that a
-- caller passes in; it is a field of the very contract the caller is exercising.
-- No extra fetch, no extra disclosure, and no extra authority delegation.
--
-- # Two interfaces
--
-- `Pausable` reports the flag and carries no authority at all. Every pausable
-- template implements it, so tooling, wallets, and auditors read one shape.
--
-- `PausableAdmin` adds the switch itself, controlled by a single `pauser`
-- party. It `requires Pausable`, so it can only be implemented on a template
-- that already implements the core, and the compiler enforces that.
--
-- The split exists because `_pause` in Solidity is `internal`: the component
-- ships no authorization and the deriving contract wires it to `Ownable` or
-- `AccessControl`. A single `Party` cannot express a role grant, an M-of-N
-- approval, or a timelock, and a controller expression is pure, so it cannot
-- fetch a role grant to decide who may act. A consumer with any of those
-- models implements `Pausable` alone and writes its own pause choice, taking
-- the caller and its credential as choice arguments:
--
-- > choice TokenRules_Pause : ContractId TokenRules
-- >   with caller : Party; grantCid : ContractId RoleGrant
-- >   controller caller
-- >   do
-- >     grant <- fetch grantCid
-- >     requireRole caller "PAUSER_ROLE" admin grant
-- >     whenNotPaused this
-- >     create this with paused = True
--
-- A consumer whose pause authority really is one party implements both and gets
-- the switch for free.
--
-- # Solidity correspondence
--
-- | `Pausable.sol`        | This module                                    |
-- |-----------------------|------------------------------------------------|
-- | `bool _paused`        | `PausableView.paused`                          |
-- | `paused()`            | `Pausable_Paused`, `isPaused`, or the view     |
-- | `whenNotPaused`       | `whenNotPaused`                                |
-- | `whenPaused`          | `whenPaused`                                   |
-- | `_pause()`            | `PausableAdmin_Pause`, or the consumer's choice|
-- | `_unpause()`          | `PausableAdmin_Unpause`, or the consumer's     |
-- | `EnforcedPause()`     | `eEnforcedPause`                               |
-- | `ExpectedPause()`     | `eExpectedPause`                               |
-- | `Paused`/`Unpaused`   | the exercise node in the transaction tree      |
--
-- Solidity emits `Paused(account)` and `Unpaused(account)` because storage
-- writes are otherwise invisible. Daml needs no such event: exercising
-- `PausableAdmin_Pause` is itself a node in the transaction tree, recorded with
-- its actor and timestamp and delivered to every stakeholder.
--
-- # Scope
--
-- This is a switch per contract. Pausing several templates at once means one
-- flag on each, or a single contract that all the protected operations are
-- exercised on.
--
-- Pause is origination control. A gated choice refuses to start while paused;
-- transactions already committed are unaffected, and a flip rejects the
-- concurrent operations that were reading the contract.
--
-- CIP-0112's `pauseInfo` fields are deliberately absent. A registry that must
-- serve `reason` and `until` on its metadata endpoint carries them as its own
-- template fields beside `paused`, so the whole response still comes from one
-- on-ledger contract without freezing those fields into this interface.
--
-- # Interfaces are frozen
--
-- Daml interfaces are not SCU-upgradeable, so this package defines interfaces
-- and no templates. Implementations live in consuming packages.
module OpenZeppelin.PausableV1 where

-- | Whether an implementing contract is currently paused.
data PausableView = PausableView with
    paused : Bool
      -- ^ Whether the contract is currently paused.
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | The read side of the switch. Implement this on any template that has a
-- pause flag, whatever authority flips it.
--
-- Signatories and observers: this interface adds none. It inherits whatever the
--   implementing template defines, which is why it costs no privacy surface.
-- Controllers: `Pausable_Paused` is controlled by its own `actor`, so any party
--   that can see the contract may read the flag.
-- Choices: `Pausable_Paused` only. Nothing here changes state.
-- Disclosure and privacy expectations: the flag is visible to exactly the
--   parties who can already see the implementing contract. Keep sensitive
--   detail about why a pause is in force out of the implementing template's
--   observable fields.
-- Authorization assumptions: none. This interface grants no authority and
--   confers no ability to pause.
-- Archival behavior: none. The one choice is nonconsuming.
-- Failure modes: none of its own. The guards below fail with `eEnforcedPause`
--   or `eExpectedPause`.
-- Upgrade and migration assumptions: frozen for the life of the package.
--   `PausableView` gains no fields.
interface Pausable where
  viewtype PausableView

  -- | Read the flag. The `paused()` getter.
  --
  -- Reading the interface view off the ledger API gives the same answer without
  -- a transaction; this choice exists for code that must read the flag from
  -- inside a workflow.
  nonconsuming choice Pausable_Paused : Bool
    with
      actor : Party
        -- ^ The party asking. Authorizes the read and appears in the tree.
    controller actor
    do
      pure (view this).paused

-- | Fail unless the contract is unpaused. The `whenNotPaused` modifier.
--
-- Accepts an implementing template, a `Pausable`, or a `PausableAdmin`, so the
-- same guard serves a consumer's choice and this module's own choices.
--
-- > choice Vault_Withdraw : ContractId Vault
-- >   with amount : Int
-- >   controller holder
-- >   do
-- >     whenNotPaused this
-- >     create this with balance = balance - amount
whenNotPaused : (HasToInterface t Pausable) => t -> Update ()
whenNotPaused x = assertMsg eEnforcedPause (not (isPaused x))

-- | Fail unless the contract is paused. The `whenPaused` modifier.
-- Use it on recovery choices that may run only during an incident.
whenPaused : (HasToInterface t Pausable) => t -> Update ()
whenPaused x = assertMsg eExpectedPause (isPaused x)

-- | `paused()` as a pure function, for a choice that branches on the flag
-- rather than refusing to run.
isPaused : (HasToInterface t Pausable) => t -> Bool
isPaused x = (view (toInterface @Pausable x)).paused

-- | The pause authority, for the single-party case.
data PausableAdminView = PausableAdminView with
    pauser : Party
      -- ^ The one party that may pause and unpause.
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | The write side of the switch, for a template whose pause authority is one
-- party. Optional: implement it to inherit `PausableAdmin_Pause` and
-- `PausableAdmin_Unpause` instead of writing them.
--
-- A consumer whose authority is a role, a party set, or a timelock implements
-- `Pausable` alone and writes its own pause choice. Do not implement this
-- interface with a placeholder `pauser`: the choices below really work for that
-- party, so a misleading value is a live second path to the switch.
--
-- Signatories and observers: inherited from the implementing template.
-- Controllers: `pauser` controls both choices.
-- Choices: `PausableAdmin_Pause` and `PausableAdmin_Unpause`.
-- Disclosure and privacy expectations: `pauser` is published in the view, so
--   every party who can see the contract learns who holds the switch.
-- Authorization assumptions: only `pauser` may flip the flag. The implementing
--   template's own signatories authorize the successor contract.
-- Archival behavior: both choices are consuming. They archive the current
--   contract and create its successor through `setPausedImpl`.
-- Failure modes: pausing while paused fails with `eEnforcedPause`; unpausing
--   while unpaused fails with `eExpectedPause`. This matches Solidity, where
--   `_pause` carries `whenNotPaused` and `_unpause` carries `whenPaused`.
-- Upgrade and migration assumptions: frozen for the life of the package.
interface PausableAdmin requires Pausable where
  viewtype PausableAdminView

  -- | Re-create the implementing contract with a new value of the flag.
  --
  -- The one obligation an implementer takes on. It must preserve every other
  -- field, so that pausing changes the flag and nothing else:
  --
  -- > setPausedImpl newPaused =
  -- >   toInterfaceContractId <$> create this with paused = newPaused
  setPausedImpl : Bool -> Update (ContractId PausableAdmin)

  -- | Pause the contract. Fails if it is already paused.
  choice PausableAdmin_Pause : ContractId PausableAdmin
    controller (view this).pauser
    do
      whenNotPaused this
      setPausedImpl this True

  -- | Unpause the contract. Fails if it is not paused.
  choice PausableAdmin_Unpause : ContractId PausableAdmin
    controller (view this).pauser
    do
      whenPaused this
      setPausedImpl this False

-- Error messages. The Solidity custom errors, as text.

-- | Raised when an operation requires the contract to be unpaused.
eEnforcedPause : Text
eEnforcedPause = "Pausable: enforced pause"

-- | Raised when an operation requires the contract to be paused.
eExpectedPause : Text
eExpectedPause = "Pausable: expected pause"
